182
00:08:28,620 --> 00:08:30,700
Right, so what is Julia?

183
00:08:30,700 --> 00:08:35,850
So Julia development started
right here at MIT at 2009.

184
00:08:35,850 --> 00:08:39,240
That was the very early
days, and version 1.0

185
00:08:39,240 --> 00:08:45,115
got released almost 10 years
later in August of 2018.

186
00:08:45,115 --> 00:08:47,490
And Julia has been growing
basically throughout that time

187
00:08:47,490 --> 00:08:51,260
and from the 2018 onwards, and
we'll see some numbers on that.

188
00:08:51,260 --> 00:08:54,850
So July is a dynamic
interactive language.

189
00:08:54,850 --> 00:08:58,090
It's intuitive, and it's
expressive, where you can easily

190
00:08:58,090 --> 00:09:00,350
write high level syntax.

191
00:09:00,350 --> 00:09:03,400
And essentially, by
high level syntax,

192
00:09:03,400 --> 00:09:07,480
I mean anything that is
not close to machine code

193
00:09:07,480 --> 00:09:10,120
as, for instance, C is.

194
00:09:10,120 --> 00:09:13,520
And it's built upon types
and multiple dispatch.

195
00:09:13,520 --> 00:09:16,490
So we'll talk a lot about
types and multiple dispatch.

196
00:09:16,490 --> 00:09:19,160
It's two very core
concepts of Julia.

197
00:09:19,160 --> 00:09:22,090
And one way to think of
that, of this design,

198
00:09:22,090 --> 00:09:25,300
is an alternative to the
object-oriented programming

199
00:09:25,300 --> 00:09:28,780
design that, for
instance, Python has.

200
00:09:28,780 --> 00:09:31,120
And you will see more
of this both today

201
00:09:31,120 --> 00:09:32,780
in the tutorial
but also tomorrow.

202
00:09:32,780 --> 00:09:36,550
And yeah, Helmut also mentioned
this phrase, which Julia works

203
00:09:36,550 --> 00:09:39,040
like Python and runs like C.

204
00:09:39,040 --> 00:09:42,110
Essentially, this phrase sums
up all the previous points.

205
00:09:42,110 --> 00:09:46,520
And by that, we mean that
it's an expressive language.

206
00:09:46,520 --> 00:09:47,420
It's easy to write.

207
00:09:47,420 --> 00:09:51,180
But also, you can make it very
performant with a few extra

208
00:09:51,180 --> 00:09:53,290
steps if you take them.

209
00:09:53,290 --> 00:09:56,170
And also, just to get this out
of the way on the first slide,

210
00:09:56,170 --> 00:09:58,382
Julia is one-based indexing.

211
00:09:58,382 --> 00:10:00,090
If you're coming from
Python, yeah, there

212
00:10:00,090 --> 00:10:02,100
might be some shortcomings.

213
00:10:02,100 --> 00:10:04,270
I find it hard sometimes
to switch between the two.

214
00:10:04,270 --> 00:10:07,180
But basically, indexing
in Julia begins from one.

215
00:10:07,180 --> 00:10:09,030
And by this, I mean
in any collection

216
00:10:09,030 --> 00:10:13,540
of elements the first element
is index one in Julia,

217
00:10:13,540 --> 00:10:16,080
similar to MATLAB.

218
00:10:16,080 --> 00:10:20,940
Right, so I'd like to go
through all these points, all

219
00:10:20,940 --> 00:10:23,320
these features of Julia
that I mentioned one by one.

220
00:10:23,320 --> 00:10:25,230
And first, what I
mean about Julia

221
00:10:25,230 --> 00:10:28,080
being dynamic and
interactive is that Julia

222
00:10:28,080 --> 00:10:29,860
is a compiled language.

223
00:10:29,860 --> 00:10:32,940
But you can very
easily work on it

224
00:10:32,940 --> 00:10:34,780
in a dynamic environment
where, you know,

225
00:10:34,780 --> 00:10:36,280
you write lines of code.

226
00:10:36,280 --> 00:10:37,680
You evaluate those
lines of code,

227
00:10:37,680 --> 00:10:43,290
and you move on to the next one
similar to Python and MATLAB.

228
00:10:43,290 --> 00:10:46,900
And here are three interfaces
where you can do that.

229
00:10:46,900 --> 00:10:48,650
First one is the REPL.

230
00:10:48,650 --> 00:10:53,110
The REPL stands for Read
Evaluate Print Loop.

231
00:10:53,110 --> 00:10:55,550
Yeah, it's exactly what
it says on the tin.

232
00:10:55,550 --> 00:10:58,610
You write your code
line by line by line.

233
00:10:58,610 --> 00:11:01,940
The Julia compiler reads
that code and evaluates it,

234
00:11:01,940 --> 00:11:06,610
and it gives you a result
immediately on the next line.

235
00:11:06,610 --> 00:11:09,430
This is easy to do, especially
if you're prototyping

236
00:11:09,430 --> 00:11:11,990
or if you want to check
something very quickly.

237
00:11:11,990 --> 00:11:18,020
Maybe harder to develop longer
pieces of code just in the REPL.

238
00:11:18,020 --> 00:11:20,570
But then you can move on
to something like VS Code.

239
00:11:20,570 --> 00:11:25,370
VS Code has a Julia extension
which I think works great.

240
00:11:25,370 --> 00:11:30,310
So there, you can see an example
that as a person is typing

241
00:11:30,310 --> 00:11:36,430
a command, you have the
auto fill of Julia functions

242
00:11:36,430 --> 00:11:38,900
that start with M-A--
for instance, map.

243
00:11:38,900 --> 00:11:41,890
And then next to that,
you also see a panel that

244
00:11:41,890 --> 00:11:43,780
gives you the docstring of map.

245
00:11:43,780 --> 00:11:45,540
That is, you know--

246
00:11:45,540 --> 00:11:47,800
that is fetched from
Julia's documentation.

247
00:11:47,800 --> 00:11:51,250
So it tells you exactly what
map is doing as you type.

248
00:11:51,250 --> 00:11:55,530
And also, it's very
handy for plotting

249
00:11:55,530 --> 00:12:00,580
because it's time that you
plot something with your code.

250
00:12:00,580 --> 00:12:02,655
Then, a second panel
will open here,

251
00:12:02,655 --> 00:12:04,030
and you will see
that plot there.

252
00:12:04,030 --> 00:12:06,570
And you can even flip
between the history of plots

253
00:12:06,570 --> 00:12:09,910
in your current session.

254
00:12:09,910 --> 00:12:11,770
So it has this
history component.

255
00:12:11,770 --> 00:12:16,170
And also, there is a
REPL open on the bottom.

256
00:12:16,170 --> 00:12:19,450
This REPL on the bottom part is
exactly the same as that REPL.

257
00:12:19,450 --> 00:12:21,610
So you can even type
commands directly there,

258
00:12:21,610 --> 00:12:25,620
or you can evaluate your
commands from the script

259
00:12:25,620 --> 00:12:28,390
that you have there.

260
00:12:28,390 --> 00:12:30,280
And you can evaluate
them one by one,

261
00:12:30,280 --> 00:12:31,900
or you can run
the entire script.

262
00:12:31,900 --> 00:12:37,920
And then you're just going
to get final result printed.

263
00:12:37,920 --> 00:12:41,010
And a third option, which
is actually the main one

264
00:12:41,010 --> 00:12:46,150
that we're going to use for this
course, is Jupyter Notebooks.

265
00:12:46,150 --> 00:12:48,830
You might be familiar with
Jupyter Notebooks from Python.

266
00:12:48,830 --> 00:12:51,590
This is exactly
the same structure.

267
00:12:51,590 --> 00:12:55,270
Essentially, Jupyter itself
supports both Julia and Python

268
00:12:55,270 --> 00:12:57,670
and R too.

269
00:12:57,670 --> 00:13:01,820
And in a Jupyter notebook,
it's a handy interface,

270
00:13:01,820 --> 00:13:05,050
especially if you have lengthy
pieces of text that are longer

271
00:13:05,050 --> 00:13:07,010
than a single line comment.

272
00:13:07,010 --> 00:13:10,220
So as you can see there,
we can have any text,

273
00:13:10,220 --> 00:13:14,950
and we can have text blocks
and code blocks interspersed

274
00:13:14,950 --> 00:13:16,430
in one notebook.

275
00:13:16,430 --> 00:13:21,057
And this is exactly what every
page on our website looks like,

276
00:13:21,057 --> 00:13:23,140
and this is exactly the
same in the notebooks when

277
00:13:23,140 --> 00:13:25,450
you open them.

278
00:13:25,450 --> 00:13:28,570
So I would recommend everybody
using those notebooks

279
00:13:28,570 --> 00:13:29,140
that we have.

280
00:13:29,140 --> 00:13:30,890
But of course, you're
not limited to them.

281
00:13:30,890 --> 00:13:33,790
So for instance, if you have
VS Code already installed,

282
00:13:33,790 --> 00:13:37,640
it's one extra step to install
the Julia extension in VS Code.

283
00:13:37,640 --> 00:13:41,920
And you can just copy paste the
code from the website to VS Code

284
00:13:41,920 --> 00:13:44,530
and start evaluating it there
and start running it there.

285
00:13:44,530 --> 00:13:46,950
That's completely up to
you, and we can help you

286
00:13:46,950 --> 00:13:48,430
if you want to set that up.

287
00:13:48,430 --> 00:13:50,040
But the Jupyter
Notebooks are there,

288
00:13:50,040 --> 00:13:53,220
and they're already ready.

289
00:13:53,220 --> 00:13:55,560
Now, the second
claim that I made

290
00:13:55,560 --> 00:13:58,233
is that Julia is
intuitive and expressive.

291
00:13:58,233 --> 00:14:00,150
And here, I'm just showing
you an example that

292
00:14:00,150 --> 00:14:01,870
is very much relevant to us.

293
00:14:01,870 --> 00:14:06,810
This is a function
that finds a spike

294
00:14:06,810 --> 00:14:12,060
and keeps the time for that
spike as it integrates a model.

295
00:14:12,060 --> 00:14:14,640
And we can see the
expressiveness here

296
00:14:14,640 --> 00:14:18,300
because as you can
see on top, there

297
00:14:18,300 --> 00:14:21,510
is this using unitful line,
where basically, unitful

298
00:14:21,510 --> 00:14:24,280
is a package that comes
with actual physical units.

299
00:14:24,280 --> 00:14:29,040
Right, so we can easily
import this package

300
00:14:29,040 --> 00:14:32,130
and pick three units that we
want to import from that package

301
00:14:32,130 --> 00:14:36,180
and use those units immediately
in our code almost the same

302
00:14:36,180 --> 00:14:37,320
as we would in paper.

303
00:14:37,320 --> 00:14:41,780
You can see that R is
equal to 100 megaohms.

304
00:14:41,780 --> 00:14:46,350
That's standard Julia syntax
that the Julia compiler can read

305
00:14:46,350 --> 00:14:49,160
and a human can
read very easily.

306
00:14:49,160 --> 00:14:50,430
And even within the loop--

307
00:14:50,430 --> 00:14:50,930
sorry.

308
00:14:50,930 --> 00:14:54,050
Within the first
spike function, we

309
00:14:54,050 --> 00:14:56,870
have this extra expressiveness
because we're not

310
00:14:56,870 --> 00:15:00,690
limited by some rules that
other languages impose.

311
00:15:00,690 --> 00:15:06,290
For instance, in MATLAB and
Python, even in R as well,

312
00:15:06,290 --> 00:15:08,420
the documentation, the
official documentation,

313
00:15:08,420 --> 00:15:12,590
will make the claim that try to
use vectorized code because this

314
00:15:12,590 --> 00:15:13,440
is faster, right?

315
00:15:13,440 --> 00:15:16,310
So if you wrap everything,
if you organize your data

316
00:15:16,310 --> 00:15:19,160
into matrices and vectors
and doing operations

317
00:15:19,160 --> 00:15:22,550
with linear algebra,
basically, that's

318
00:15:22,550 --> 00:15:25,220
going to be faster than looping
over element by element.

319
00:15:25,220 --> 00:15:27,050
In Julia, that's not the case.

320
00:15:27,050 --> 00:15:30,230
So you can use vectorized
operations or you can use loops,

321
00:15:30,230 --> 00:15:34,200
and the performance
will be almost the same.

322
00:15:34,200 --> 00:15:38,097
So you have this
extra expressiveness.

323
00:15:38,097 --> 00:15:39,930
It depends from person
to person, of course,

324
00:15:39,930 --> 00:15:41,722
but I would make the
claim that it's easier

325
00:15:41,722 --> 00:15:45,630
to read out a loop versus
reading a matrix multiplication

326
00:15:45,630 --> 00:15:48,600
and figuring out exactly
what is happening.

327
00:15:48,600 --> 00:15:54,390
But also, the fact that Julia is
expressive comes from the fact

328
00:15:54,390 --> 00:15:58,740
that it allows you to
build to essentially design

329
00:15:58,740 --> 00:16:02,310
your own syntax on top
of the language syntax

330
00:16:02,310 --> 00:16:03,340
that is already there.

331
00:16:03,340 --> 00:16:06,000
And that is through macros.

332
00:16:06,000 --> 00:16:09,870
So macros help you make those
domain-specific languages

333
00:16:09,870 --> 00:16:10,840
within Julia.

334
00:16:10,840 --> 00:16:12,990
For instance, on
the bottom left,

335
00:16:12,990 --> 00:16:14,550
you can see
differential equations

336
00:16:14,550 --> 00:16:19,710
where you can type in a system
of differential equations

337
00:16:19,710 --> 00:16:21,090
symbolically.

338
00:16:21,090 --> 00:16:23,950
That comes from modeling toolkit
that Helmut mentioned as well.

339
00:16:23,950 --> 00:16:27,840
So Julia is able to
parse those symbols,

340
00:16:27,840 --> 00:16:33,660
and it eventually creates this
differential equation system

341
00:16:33,660 --> 00:16:35,160
because of these
macros that you can

342
00:16:35,160 --> 00:16:36,827
see-- the at variables
and at parameters

343
00:16:36,827 --> 00:16:38,720
for instance, and at equations.

344
00:16:38,720 --> 00:16:41,130
Those things tell the
compiler that, you know,

345
00:16:41,130 --> 00:16:45,660
now you're going to see a block
of symbolic code with maybe,

346
00:16:45,660 --> 00:16:47,960
you know, some symbols
and some default

347
00:16:47,960 --> 00:16:52,670
values or some equations
split by the tilde symbol.

348
00:16:52,670 --> 00:16:56,640
So this extends the
language capabilities a lot.

349
00:16:56,640 --> 00:16:59,750
And similarly, if you want
to, for instance solve,

350
00:16:59,750 --> 00:17:02,000
some optimization
problem, you can use Jump.

351
00:17:02,000 --> 00:17:05,030
And in Jump, you can use
a similar very high level

352
00:17:05,030 --> 00:17:07,670
syntax of, you know, first
you start with a model.

353
00:17:07,670 --> 00:17:09,829
And your model, you
pick your optimizer.

354
00:17:09,829 --> 00:17:13,349
And then you just iteratively
add stuff to your model.

355
00:17:13,349 --> 00:17:15,109
For instance, you
add the variable x,

356
00:17:15,109 --> 00:17:18,079
and x is strictly positive--

357
00:17:18,079 --> 00:17:19,790
sorry, non-negative
in this case.

358
00:17:19,790 --> 00:17:23,849
You add a y that is
bounded from both ends.

359
00:17:23,849 --> 00:17:25,109
You add an objective function.

360
00:17:25,109 --> 00:17:26,223
You add some constraint,

361
00:17:26,223 --> 00:17:27,890
And at the end, you
can print the model,

362
00:17:27,890 --> 00:17:30,060
and you have this nice
symbolic print of your model.

363
00:17:30,060 --> 00:17:32,250
And if I were to show
you one more line,

364
00:17:32,250 --> 00:17:34,820
then you can actually solve
this model using the optimizer

365
00:17:34,820 --> 00:17:35,490
that you pick.

366
00:17:35,490 --> 00:17:38,790
And it gives you
whatever minimizes

367
00:17:38,790 --> 00:17:39,970
your objective function.

368
00:17:39,970 --> 00:17:41,167
But also, of course--

369
00:17:41,167 --> 00:17:42,750
and this is the
reason why we're here.

370
00:17:42,750 --> 00:17:45,400
Neuroblox serves this value.

371
00:17:45,400 --> 00:17:47,760
It's very easy in Neuroblox,
as you can see there

372
00:17:47,760 --> 00:17:51,550
in that small block of code,
to just define the two neurons.

373
00:17:51,550 --> 00:17:53,700
In that case we have an
excitatory Hodgkin-Huxley

374
00:17:53,700 --> 00:17:56,350
neuron, an inhibitory
Hodgkin-Huxley neuron.

375
00:17:56,350 --> 00:17:59,020
And then we create
an empty graph

376
00:17:59,020 --> 00:18:02,260
because every model in
Neuroblox starts as a graph.

377
00:18:02,260 --> 00:18:05,860
And we connect the inhibitory
neuron to the excitatory one.

378
00:18:05,860 --> 00:18:11,130
So this simple model with
two neurons connected one way

379
00:18:11,130 --> 00:18:16,350
can be defined in these
few lines of code.

380
00:18:16,350 --> 00:18:19,980
Now, the next thing, as I said,
types in multiple dispatch.

381
00:18:19,980 --> 00:18:23,970
They are at Julia's core.

382
00:18:23,970 --> 00:18:28,570
The multiple dispatch, this
is a quote from Wikipedia.

383
00:18:28,570 --> 00:18:33,220
And you know, in
more layman's terms,

384
00:18:33,220 --> 00:18:37,730
this property where you can
have the same function-- you're

385
00:18:37,730 --> 00:18:41,050
using the same name, having
different methods implemented

386
00:18:41,050 --> 00:18:41,550
for it.

387
00:18:41,550 --> 00:18:45,980
And by methods, we mean the
combinations of its arguments

388
00:18:45,980 --> 00:18:47,450
can be different.

389
00:18:47,450 --> 00:18:51,090
Some methods can have more or
less arguments than others,

390
00:18:51,090 --> 00:18:53,090
or some methods can have
exactly the same number

391
00:18:53,090 --> 00:18:55,500
of arguments as others
just with different types.

392
00:18:55,500 --> 00:19:00,050
Right, so you can see the
definition of an f function,

393
00:19:00,050 --> 00:19:01,620
having two methods, right?

394
00:19:01,620 --> 00:19:05,420
The first method works with
only floating point neurons

395
00:19:05,420 --> 00:19:09,920
of-- or, sorry, floating point
numbers with 64-bit precision.

396
00:19:09,920 --> 00:19:11,480
And then we define
the second method

397
00:19:11,480 --> 00:19:12,930
that works with any number.

398
00:19:12,930 --> 00:19:20,340
Number is an abstract
type over floating floats.

399
00:19:20,340 --> 00:19:25,310
So as you can see, when we
call f using a float, 2.0,

400
00:19:25,310 --> 00:19:28,790
and an integer, 3,
then the second method

401
00:19:28,790 --> 00:19:32,700
gets evaluated because that's
the best match that we have.

402
00:19:32,700 --> 00:19:35,510
But if we call f
with a 2.0 and a 3.0,

403
00:19:35,510 --> 00:19:39,430
then the first one gets called
because both of those numbers

404
00:19:39,430 --> 00:19:44,200
are floats, and they're
float 64 by default in Julia.

405
00:19:44,200 --> 00:19:47,740
And you can see proof
of that at the end.

406
00:19:47,740 --> 00:19:51,350
That symbol between float and
number, that means subtype.

407
00:19:51,350 --> 00:19:54,830
So this is just a logical
comparator of types,

408
00:19:54,830 --> 00:19:58,690
if you want to, that you
compare float to number,

409
00:19:58,690 --> 00:20:02,000
and you get true because you're
claiming that float is a--

410
00:20:02,000 --> 00:20:02,740
sorry.

411
00:20:02,740 --> 00:20:06,970
Float is a subtype of number.

412
00:20:06,970 --> 00:20:09,170
So that's why those
evaluations work.

413
00:20:09,170 --> 00:20:14,000
And now types, float 64 and
numbers, those are Julia types.

414
00:20:14,000 --> 00:20:15,650
But also you can
define your own types,

415
00:20:15,650 --> 00:20:18,590
and we will do that tomorrow
when we define our own blocks.

416
00:20:18,590 --> 00:20:20,890
And on the right
here, you can see

417
00:20:20,890 --> 00:20:25,220
an example of defining a
structure called my type.

418
00:20:25,220 --> 00:20:29,290
Structures are collections
of elements that

419
00:20:29,290 --> 00:20:33,020
are like composite data types.

420
00:20:33,020 --> 00:20:38,540
And within your structure,
you can explicitly

421
00:20:38,540 --> 00:20:44,820
cast the type on any of the
variables that the type holds.

422
00:20:44,820 --> 00:20:48,110
Or you can just leave
it blank, and it

423
00:20:48,110 --> 00:20:49,770
can take any type of input.

424
00:20:49,770 --> 00:20:53,100
For instance, here in my
type, x has no type, right?

425
00:20:53,100 --> 00:20:55,560
So x can be any type in Julia.

426
00:20:55,560 --> 00:20:59,180
Y is strictly an integer,
and z is a float 64.

427
00:20:59,180 --> 00:21:02,630
So we can see that my type,
when the first thing is a string

428
00:21:02,630 --> 00:21:06,560
called hello, perfectly works.

429
00:21:06,560 --> 00:21:10,560
And the combination of these
two concepts, you know,

430
00:21:10,560 --> 00:21:12,290
custom types with
multiple dispatch

431
00:21:12,290 --> 00:21:18,510
can extend Julia's features by
basically unpredictable amounts.

432
00:21:18,510 --> 00:21:20,970
You can do so many things.

433
00:21:20,970 --> 00:21:23,280
And you know, borrowing this
term from complex systems,

434
00:21:23,280 --> 00:21:25,550
we can see the-- you can
see emergence features

435
00:21:25,550 --> 00:21:26,220
in the language.

436
00:21:26,220 --> 00:21:27,720
And you will see
an example of that.

437
00:21:27,720 --> 00:21:30,050
And by emerging
features, I mean we

438
00:21:30,050 --> 00:21:33,760
can take types and
functions that are not

439
00:21:33,760 --> 00:21:36,970
designed to work together, but
they just work together, maybe

440
00:21:36,970 --> 00:21:40,360
by defining one more method or
maybe doing-- like, sometimes

441
00:21:40,360 --> 00:21:42,610
with adding no extra
code but sometimes

442
00:21:42,610 --> 00:21:43,842
with minimal extra code.

443
00:21:43,842 --> 00:21:46,300
So we see this unpredictable
behavior coming here and there

444
00:21:46,300 --> 00:21:50,740
that are welcome
features to have.

445
00:21:50,740 --> 00:21:53,570
Now on performance,
this is the runs

446
00:21:53,570 --> 00:21:57,400
like C parts of
that famous mantra.

447
00:21:57,400 --> 00:21:59,470
Here, I'm just showing
you some benchmark

448
00:21:59,470 --> 00:22:02,330
with popular functions.

449
00:22:02,330 --> 00:22:04,690
Some of them come
from linear algebra,

450
00:22:04,690 --> 00:22:07,870
like some matrix multiplication
or matrix statistics.

451
00:22:07,870 --> 00:22:09,260
Some of them are
just recursions,

452
00:22:09,260 --> 00:22:12,770
like the recursive calculator
of the Fibonacci sequence.

453
00:22:12,770 --> 00:22:15,790
And as you can see,
on the x-axis here,

454
00:22:15,790 --> 00:22:17,500
we have different languages.

455
00:22:17,500 --> 00:22:19,430
And they're all compared to C.

456
00:22:19,430 --> 00:22:21,230
That's why C is
collapsed in one point.

457
00:22:21,230 --> 00:22:23,800
And as you can see,
Julia is very much

458
00:22:23,800 --> 00:22:28,760
approximating C behavior in
most of those benchmarks.

459
00:22:28,760 --> 00:22:32,090
And you can see this is in stark
contrast with other high level

460
00:22:32,090 --> 00:22:37,400
languages like
MATLAB, R, Octave.

